WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Math assumes you can see it and hold a pencil.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:10.000
For blind and low-vision students, that's the barrier — not ability. This is Glance.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:18.000
A student uploads a worksheet. Glance reads it into real, structured math,

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:25.000
and moves straight to the first line.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:35.000
Before the details, the shape: "an equation; on the left, two terms."

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:45.000
Then they explore it piece by piece with the arrow keys.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:53.000
Now the work. They hold Space and say their step.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Glance reads it back, and nothing is saved until they confirm it.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.000
In homework mode, algebra software checks the step.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:10.000
Glance never solves the problem, and never says "wrong" — only whether a step checks out.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Spoken math is ambiguous. "x plus one over two" could mean two things,

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:25.000
so Glance asks instead of guessing.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:35.000
Low-vision students can read one line at a time, as large as they need.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:45.000
When they're done, they hand in typed work their instructor and their screen reader can both read.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:50.000
Glance. Math you can hear and speak.
